 { White played 1.Ng5 here - why was that a mistake? What was preferable? } 1. { -KEY- } Nf6 $1 { The correct continuation. White should bet on the a-pawn while also keeping the rooks on the board. } ( { a) Let's examine the alternatives: After } 1. a5 $2 Rh5 2. Kd4 Rxa5 3. Nf6 Ra8 4. Rc7 Bg8 5. Rg7 Rf8 6. Ke5 Rxf6 $1 7. Kxf6 Bh7 { a first type of theoretical draw emerges. Thanks to her bishop being correctly placed on the b1-h7 diagonal, Black defends comfortably; e.g. } 8. Rc7 Bd3 9. Rd7 Bc2 ) 
 ( { b) Roughly the same thing occurs after } 1. Kf5 $2 Rxe4 $1 2. Kxe4 Bxa4 { At this point, Black just needs to bring her bishop to the b1-h7 diagonal in order to secure a draw, and after } 3. Rc7 Bb5 { it is not clear how White can prevent that. If the rook leaves the seventh rank then ...Kh7 becomes possible, and if it moves to d7 Black expels it by ...Bb5, while king moves permit ...Bd3. } ) 
 ( { c) As for } 1. Rb6 $2 { Black should prefer } Rh5 $1 ( { avoiding } 1. Nf6 Bxa4 $2 2. Ng5 $1 ) 
2. Kf4 Bxa4 3. Rb8 Kh7 4. Nf6 Kxh6 5. Rh8 Kg7 $1 6. Rxh5 Kxf6 { with another type of theoretical draw. } ) 
 ( { d) Equally unsuccessful is } 1. Rc8 $2 Kh7 2. Nf6 Kxh6 3. Rh8 Kg5 4. Rxh4 ( { or } 4. Ne4 Rxe4 $1 5. Kxe4 Bxa4 ) 
Kxh4 5. a5 { due to } Bc4 6. Kd4 Be2 ( 6. Nf6 Bf1 { works as well; } ) 
7. Nd5 Kg5 { with a position that occurred in the old game H. Bird-G.Maróczy, London 1899. There followed } 8. Kc5 Kf5 9. Nc3 Bf1 10. Nb5 Ke6 11. a6 Kd7 12. a7 Bg2 { and Maróczy made a draw without much effort. } ) 
 ( { e) } 1. Ng5 $2 { This allows Black to save herself on the theme of a theoretical draw. } Rxa4 $1 { Having seen a tactical resource two moves further on, Stefanova exploits the moment to eliminate the dangerous a-pawn. } 2. Rc8 Bg8 3. h7 Ra5 $1 { Perhaps this check was overlooked by Qin when playing 1 Ng5. } 4. Kf4 Rxg5 $1 5. Kxg5 Kxh7 { Draw agreed. } ( { Thanks to her king being located in a safe corner, Black holds; e.g. } 5. Nf6 Kxh7 6. Rc7 Kh8 7. Kh6 Bd5 ) 
 ) 
Rxh6 ( { There was the threat of } 1... Rxh6 1. Rc8 ) 
2. a5 { Here it is difficult to see how Black can avoid the arrival of the pawn at a7, and her king is worryingly far away. Without much need to dig further into this position (Chessable user FSU70 actually did so, and concluded that the position after 2. a5 is indeed a tablebase win for White), we can conclude that 1. Nf6, despite being the shortest line, is the one that causes most problems for Black. } 
